ns like  karaoke lounges and casinos have been  closed  the streets of mecca are once again  filling up with muslim pilgrims as saudi  arabia welcomes international visitors  for the hajj pilgrimage for the first  time since the beginning of the pandemic  more than one million people are  expected to take part in the five-day  ritual a big jump from the 60 000 who  attended last year but still  significantly fewer than the 2.5 million  in 2019.  in the 133 days since russia's invasion  of ukraine the united nations refugee  agency says 9 million people have fled  the country  those numbers are expected to grow the  governor of donetsk is calling on the  region's 350 thousand remaining  residents to leave as russian forces  continue to take aim at markets homes  and other civilian targets southwest of  there video from local emergency  services shows the situation in miko  live where the city's mayor says a  residential area was hit by rockets late  tuesday  worldwide the number of people dealing  with hunger on a daily basis is climbing  rapidly a new united nations report  found in 2021 more than 820 million  people were living with food insecurity  and frequent nutrition deficits that's  an increase of about 150 million people  since the outbreak of the pandemic the  report warns the situation will only get  worse due to the ongoing supply chain  challenges and the war in ukraine a  country which previously produced a huge  amount of grain for export  blowing the whistle ahead why some  former group home workers claimed  children's lives were put at risk  in may with no public announcement child  welfare company connor holmes  surrendered its license to operate  foster care homes while the ontario  government says connor homes is unfit to  run foster care homes the company is  still allowed to operate group homes our  team at global news in partnership with  aptn has also been looking into connor  homes our carolyn jarvis joins us now  from toronto carolyn  colleen while we were investigating  connor holmes management sent this email  to staff saying no one can make public  statements to the media not now or ever  but the former workers you're about to  hear from are blowing the whistle saying  some kids are being put in danger  it's awful it's just money focused if  they cared about the kids you never saw  it they should be shut down we have  agreed to hide the identities of these  former connor holmes workers because of  the risk of reprisal still they insisted  their message be heard i just felt like  i was creating monsters out of the kids  for 46 years connor homes operated  foster care and group homes and it still  runs three group homes in eastern  ontario  a for-profit business funded in part by  taxpayer dollars i feel like they just  cared about the money and not the kids  former workers say making money meant  cutting costs one of the youth was going  to school and connor homes put their  schoolwork in a grocery bag as if the  kid needs any other type of sign that  they're a group home kid workers say  christmas gifts were mostly clothing  instead of toys because budgets were too  tight to afford both the youth were  lower than anybody and management had  this power trip the imbalance of power  was seen in many ways say workers  including the unjustified use of  physical restraints  a practice the government only allows if  a child is at imminent risk of hurting  themselves or others were they telling  you to restrain when there was no  imminent risk that's correct we were  told to restrain them if they break a  plate according to the ministry of  children community and social services  breaking the rules isn't a new thing for  connor holmes  in 2017 a fire at one of its homes  killed a youth and a worker  two separate investigations found issues  at the home including a window made of  plexiglas blocking an escape route  though conor holmes was found not to  have violated the fire code  four years later at a different home  ministry inspectors reported plexiglas  was being used again asking is the fire  marshal aware they would just put that  there as a quick fix to make sure they  didn't have to pay for a new window  inspectors noted the plexiglas issue was  addressed  passing an inspection was critical to  having connor's license renewed and in  secretly recorded audio from september  2020 a manager is heard preparing staff  for a ministry visit what is a level two  serious occurrence  everybody has questions  workers who say they were put on the job  with as little as three hours of  training at an orientation  didn't have the answers to basic  questions  what do you say to the on-call manager i  don't know i've never recorded one  honestly  conor holmes declined an interview but  said in a statement workers receive  regular training and the company strives  to exceed the legislated minimum  regulatory standards and yet former  workers say the kids who live there were  worse off when they left most are still  in trouble with the law or drugs they  just go down bad paths because nobody  cared about them so why should they care  about themselves carolyn jarvis global  news  and we continue our investigation into  connor homes tomorrow night with a deep  dive into the company's finances asking  are taxpayers dollars reaching kids in  need  hanging on to dear life next the  terrifying chaos for a construction  worker that's caught on camera  clearly something went terribly wrong a  construction worker found himself  dangling from a construction crane in  toronto high off the ground as mike  derle reports the whole ordeal was  captured on camera and before we show  you this video you should know the man  is okay  oh my god hang on  just let your heart out  it's unclear how high the man was up in  the air but if you look closely and  listen it takes three seconds for his  hard hat to hit the ground  the man was clearly terrified  yet as the video began circulating  online torontonians weren't exactly sure  how to react was it an accident a stunt  a scene from a movie  it sure seemed real  oh my god how the [ __ ] did this happen  but remember crane girl five years ago a  young woman climbed up on a crane to  snap some selfies before needing rescue  crews to help her get down  this it soon became apparent was not  that stop the construction company  reports the man got entangled with a  tagline after hooking a load and the  crane operator failed to notice  fortunately he was safely lowered to the  work surface and not seriously injured  the incident is under investigation the  company said in a statement  the ministry of labor said the worker  did suffer injuries reportedly a broken  wrist as he hung on for his life a few  minutes of abject terror confusion  and in the end relief  microlight global news toronto  holy smoke and that is global national  for this wednesday night i'm colleen  christie tonight's your canada is kenora  ontario we love to see your canada  please email your photos to viewers at  globalnational.com  thanks for watching hope to see you back  here tomorrow good night
 

